# Barkpark Caddy maintenance handler — drop this INSIDE the site block that
# reverse-proxies the app (right after the `reverse_proxy …:4000` line). While
# the backend is unreachable (the seconds-long restart window of a deploy),
# Caddy serves this branded 503 + Retry-After instead of a raw 502.
#
# The STATUS LIST is load-bearing. A bare `handle_errors {` catches every error
# the SITE raises — including the 404 a `file_server` raises on a miss inside an
# armed `handle_path /sites/<slug>/*` static-site route. Measured on guerrilla:
# every miss on every spawned static site answered this 503 instead of 404, so
# the whole 4xx surface of the static sites was indistinguishable from a deploy
# window. `handle_errors 502 503 504` keeps the branded page on the gateway
# errors it is for (dial failure / gateway timeout) and lets a 404 be a 404.
# A 4xx/5xx the app RETURNS is proxied through untouched either way. Do NOT
# shorten that to "this never masks a real application error" — the handler
# fires on errors CADDY raises, and a `file_server` 404 IS one of those, which
# is the entire reason the status list above exists. The reassuring sentence is
# what made the incident invisible for the life of the fix.
#
# The Content-Type header is load-bearing and NOT decoration. `respond` with a
# body and no Content-Type defaults the response to `text/plain; charset=utf-8`
# (measured, Caddy 2.11.4), so the browser paints the raw `<!doctype html>…`
# source of this page instead of rendering it. Setting it changes only HOW the
# 503 renders — the STATUS stays 503 + Retry-After on every path, assets
# included, and a healthy upstream never enters this handler at all.
#
# deploy/instance-deploy.sh injects this automatically (idempotent, validated,
# auto-reverting) and repairs an already-armed box that still carries the bare
# form. For a hand-managed Caddyfile, paste the block below into each site and
# `caddy reload`.
#
# WHAT THE OTHER RENDERERS ACTUALLY EMIT, as of 2026-09-17 — this file used to
# claim they "bake the same block", and they do not. `MaintenanceHandler`
# (internal/caddyfile/caddyfile.go), its four call sites
# (caddyfile.go's reverse-proxy blocks, internal/cli/setup/caddy.go,
# internal/provisioner/attach_domain.go), and the provisioning asset
# internal/cli/setup/assets/deploy.sh + its byte-identical twin at the repo root
# ALL still emit the BARE form. A box provisioned by `bp setup` and never
# touched by instance-deploy.sh keeps it indefinitely, and deploy/site-deploy.sh
# then arms a `handle_path /sites/<slug>/*` file_server route into that same
# block — the exact nesting the incident above describes.
#
# `deploy/caddy-handle-errors-scope-check.sh` is the standing predicate over
# EVERY tracked file (not a list of renderers anyone has to remember to update).
# It names those sites on every run under a dated stand-down that expires
# 2026-09-24; a bare emission anywhere else reds immediately.
#
# NOT A DEFECT, DO NOT "FIX": caddyfile.go's isStatic() branch deliberately
# omits MaintenanceHandler entirely, so a static site's own 404 surfaces. That
# half is correctly fenced.
#
# Example site block:
#
#   guerrilla.barkpark.cloud {
#       reverse_proxy localhost:4000
#       handle_errors 502 503 504 {
#           header Retry-After "15"
#           header Content-Type "text/html; charset=utf-8"
#           respond 503 {
#               body <<BARKPARK_MAINTENANCE
#   <!doctype html> … BARKPARK_MAINTENANCE
#               close
#           }
#       }
#   }
handle_errors 502 503 504 {
	header Retry-After "15"
	header Content-Type "text/html; charset=utf-8"
	respond 503 {
		body <<BARKPARK_MAINTENANCE
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Back in a moment</title>
<style>body{font-family:system-ui,-apple-system,"Segoe UI",Roboto,sans-serif;background:#0f1115;color:#e7e9ee;display:grid;place-items:center;min-height:100vh;margin:0}.card{max-width:32rem;padding:2.5rem;text-align:center}h1{font-size:1.5rem;margin:0 0 .5rem}p{opacity:.7;line-height:1.5}.spinner{width:2rem;height:2rem;border:3px solid #2a2f3a;border-top-color:#6ea8fe;border-radius:50%;margin:0 auto 1.5rem;animation:spin 1s linear infinite}@keyframes spin{to{transform:rotate(360deg)}}</style>
</head><body><div class="card"><div class="spinner"></div><h1>Back in a moment</h1>
<p>Barkpark is deploying an update and will be right back. This page refreshes automatically.</p></div>
<script>setTimeout(function(){location.reload()},15000)</script></body></html>
BARKPARK_MAINTENANCE
		close
	}
}
